Subject: fonts: subpixel positioning follows the claimed platform's rule, not the field

Patch 0122 said that `subpixel_positioning` false makes a Linux persona's
advances integral, "which is exactly what a Linux desktop at scale factor 1
reports", and measured 315 for a 16px Helvetica pangram under the Linux
persona on a Mac. A Linux desktop does not report that. Stock Google Chrome
151.0.7922.137 on a real Linux desktop at device scale factor 1 (fontconfig
hintslight, the near-universal setting patch 0117 cites) reports 316.6015625
for the same pangram -- the identical font-unit fraction a Mac reports, and
the same on this fork's own linux-x64 build on that desktop. So v0.2.0 gave a
Linux persona on a macOS host an advance no Linux Chrome produces, on the one
text surface every metrics fingerprint reads first.

The tuple was right; the consumer was wrong. `gfx::FontRenderParams::
subpixel_positioning` is false on an unscaled Linux desktop
(font_render_params_linux.cc:267), but what reaches `SkFont::setSubpixel`
there is not that field. Blink's Linux path force-enables subpixel
positioning unless hinting is full:

    bool force_subpixel_positioning = !WebTestSupport::IsRunningWebTest() &&
                                      sk_hint_style != SkFontHinting::kFull;
    font->setSubpixel(force_subpixel_positioning || use_subpixel_positioning);
    font->setLinearMetrics(use_subpixel_positioning == 1);

(web_font_render_style.cc:106-113). With slight hinting that is true, so
`SkFontGetGlyphWidthForHarfBuzz` never rounds and every advance stays a
fraction. Windows never reads the field at all: font_platform_data_win.cc:
67-69 sets `setSubpixel(true)` whenever the text is antialiased. macOS
hard-codes true. Only 0122's macOS `CreateSkFont` fed the field straight into
`setSubpixel`, and that is the one line that rounds.

So the flag is derived here the way the claimed platform derives it:

    Linux     setSubpixel(hinting != full || subpixel_positioning)
              setLinearMetrics(subpixel_positioning)
    Windows   setSubpixel(antialiasing)
              setLinearMetrics(false)         -- never set on Windows
    macOS     setSubpixel(true), setLinearMetrics(true)  -- unchanged

The web-test override keeps clearing both together, as before. Linear
metrics are inert on a CoreText-backed typeface either way; they are mirrored
so that the FreeType-backed typefaces patch 0049 hands a persona off its own
OS see the same pair that OS sets.

The `check` on coh.text-advance-subpixel-quantisation encoded the same wrong
belief -- `is_fractional == (claimed_os == 'macOS' or
profile.font_subpixel_positioning)` -- and is corrected beside this patch
with the measurement as T0.

MEASURED on the macos-arm64 build carrying this patch, 16px Helvetica pangram,
`measureText` / `getBoundingClientRect`:

    persona   measureText   client rect   DPR
    linux     316.6015625   316.609375    1
    windows   316.6015625   316.609375    1
    macos     316.6015625   316.6015625   2
    host      316.6015625   316.609375    1

Stock Chrome on the Linux desktop: 316.6015625 / 316.609375 at DPR 1. Stock
Chrome on this Mac, real window at DPR 2: 316.6015625 / 316.6015625. The rect
follows the DPR's layout snapping on every row, and the Linux persona now
matches the Linux desktop on both numbers; before this patch it measured 315
and 315. /tmp/w2/FontRender/probe.js with its Linux assertion corrected to
the measured rule: 23 checks pass on this build, and the same corrected probe
fails exactly that assertion on the released v0.2.0 binary. The windows and
macos personas are byte-identical to their pre-patch baselines (canvas
df91c47fa322e806, the recorded advances), the Linux persona's raster still
moves (19d0b36eae09f0c4) and is stable across launches, and the
-webkit-font-smoothing oracle is unchanged. Georgia under the Linux persona
resolves to the same fallback advance the Linux desktop reports
(511.65625), which is the font axis agreeing with this one.

Not measured: a Windows host. Its own `CreateSkFont` never consulted the
field, so it was not affected and is not changed.

Ledger: fonts.text-advances, coh.text-advance-subpixel-quantisation,
coh.measuretext-vs-clientrects, emit.blink.font-platform-data-create-skfont-mac.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/mac/font_platform_data_mac.mm
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/mac/font_platform_data_mac.mm
@@ -257,9 +257,34 @@
   bool should_smooth_fonts =
       composed ? composed->subpixel_rendering != "none" : true;
   bool should_antialias = composed ? composed->antialiasing : true;
-  bool should_subpixel_position =
-      composed ? composed->subpixel_positioning : true;
 
+  // Whether the SkFont positions glyphs at fractional x is not the tuple's
+  // subpixel_positioning field on any platform; it is that platform's own
+  // rule over the tuple, and the rule is what decides whether every advance
+  // in the browser is a font-unit fraction or a whole pixel
+  // (skia_text_metrics.cc rounds when !isSubpixel()). Linux force-enables it
+  // unless the hinting is full (web_font_render_style.cc:106-111), so a
+  // desktop at scale factor 1 with hintslight -- subpixel_positioning false --
+  // still measures 316.6015625 for 16px Helvetica, which a stock Chrome
+  // 151.0.7922.137 on such a desktop was measured to report. Windows enables
+  // it whenever the text is antialiased and never reads the field
+  // (font_platform_data_win.cc:67-69). macOS hard-codes true. Reading the
+  // field directly instead made a Linux persona on this host round to 315,
+  // an advance no Linux Chrome produces.
+  bool should_subpixel_position = true;
+  bool linear_metrics = true;
+  if (composed && profile->ua_platform()) {
+    const std::string& platform = *profile->ua_platform();
+    if (platform == "Linux") {
+      should_subpixel_position =
+          composed->hinting != "full" || composed->subpixel_positioning;
+      linear_metrics = composed->subpixel_positioning;
+    } else if (platform == "Windows") {
+      should_subpixel_position = composed->antialiasing;
+      linear_metrics = false;
+    }
+  }
+
   // -webkit-font-smoothing reaches the SkFont on macOS and nowhere else:
   // neither font_platform_data_win.cc nor the generic font_platform_data.cc
   // reads FontDescription::FontSmoothing when it builds one. So a page that
@@ -296,6 +321,7 @@
         should_antialias && WebTestSupport::IsFontAntialiasingEnabledForTest();
     should_subpixel_position =
         WebTestSupport::IsTextSubpixelPositioningAllowedForTest();
+    linear_metrics = should_subpixel_position;
   }
 
   SkFont skfont(typeface_);
@@ -315,8 +341,11 @@
 
   // CoreText always provides linear metrics if it can, so the linear metrics
   // flag setting doesn't affect typefaces backed by CoreText. However, it
-  // does affect FreeType backed typefaces, so set the flag for consistency.
-  skfont.setLinearMetrics(should_subpixel_position);
+  // does affect FreeType backed typefaces, so set the flag for consistency:
+  // Linux sets it from the tuple's subpixel_positioning
+  // (web_font_render_style.cc:113), Windows never sets it, macOS sets it
+  // with subpixel positioning.
+  skfont.setLinearMetrics(linear_metrics);
 
   // The persona's hinting level, before the CoreGraphics override below so
   // that the override still wins. Set for the same reason the linear-metrics
